## Why This Is Asked
Test ability to count bits with a constant-time sequence of masks and shifts, no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting
- Fixed masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F...
- No branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function popcount64(x) {
  // x is a BigInt
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 128-bit using the same pattern?
- What are the performance implications on 32-bit vs 64-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to apply fixed-pattern bit-twiddling without loops, focusing on nibble-level reversal. It shows understanding of masks and shifts across 64 bits.

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise masks across 64 bits
- Fixed-sequence shifts for in-place reversal
- No branching ensures predictable performance

## Code Example
```c
uint64 nibbleReverse64(uint64 x){
  x = ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1);
  x = ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2);
  x = ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4);
  return x;
}
```

