- How would you modify for 32-bit inputs?
- Compare this to using built-ins like __builtin_ctzll in terms of portability and code size.

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses branch-free 128-bit rotation, a common primitive in crypto and low-level optimizations.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values via two 64-bit halves
- Branchless selection with masks
- Cross-half rotation logic and edge cases (n=0, n=64, n=127)

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotateLeft128(uint128 x, uint8_t n) {
  uint64_t n0 = n & 63;
  uint64_t m  = -((int)((n >> 6) & 1)); // 0 or -1
  uint64_t hi1 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo1 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t hi2 = (x.lo << n0) | (x.hi >> (64 - n0));
  uint64_t lo2 = (x.hi << n0) | (x.lo >> (64 - n0));
  uint128 r;
  r.hi = (hi1 & ~m) | (hi2 & m);
  r.lo = (lo1 & ~m) | (lo2 & m);
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How might you test for constant-time properties across inputs?
- How would you extend to a constant-time generic rotate for arbitrary bit-widths?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to compare 128-bit values split across lanes and derive a scalar metric without 128-bit math.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit data split into hi/lo lanes
- XOR to highlight differences
- 64-bit popcount for bit counting

## Code Example

```javascript
// Reference approach in C-like pseudocode; interview would use C/C++
int hammingDistance128(uint64_t a_hi, uint64_t a_lo, uint64_t b_hi, uint64_t b_lo) {
  uint64_t dh = a_hi ^ b_hi;
  uint64_t dl = a_lo ^ b_lo;
  return __builtin_popcountll(dh) + __builtin_popcountll(dl);
}
```

